> Sorry but if you don't understand Lojban then ... why all this discussion
here?

I mainly got involved because you seemed not to understand English or
linguistics, and I'd hoped my involvement might serve to enlighten.

> I was trying to show how Lojban parsers deal with another level of
analysing text.
>
> If you can't compare two languages then how can you say what's going on
in Lojban?
>
> It's a must to understand what {lo se xi vei mo'e zo'e nei} means.

It sounds like it would be useful, yes, and perhaps in due course it might
occur to you to offer an explanation of what it means.

But as I said in an earlier message, since Lojban is committed to being
monoparsing, that Lojban sentence will monoparse, and its continuing
relevance is not obvious. You explained why you thought it was the same as
the English example, and I endeavored to explain why everybody else thinks
otherwise.

--And.

